      "description": "'Gateway of the Ram Bagh- The Cutcherry Inside', by Felice Beato, Amritsar, ca.1858. This is a photograph of the Ram Bagh/Rambagh, a garden and palace complex dating to Sikh-rule. AN IMPORTANT COLLECTION OF SIX PHOTOGRAPHIC PRINTS OF THE GOLDEN TEMPLE COMPLEX, FELICE BEATO, NORTH INDIA, CIRCA 1858. Albumen prints on matte paper, set in card window mounts, with graphite inscriptions on the reverse in a 19th century hand. Largest 9¾ x 12in. (24.8 x 30cm.) (6) The prints include: a. 'The Minarets of the Ramgurrean Sirdars- Sacred Temple- South View b. 'Baba Atal's Temple' c. 'Sacred Temple- North East View' d. 'Gateway of the Ram Bagh- The Cutcherry Inside' e. 'Street- Inside Sacred Tak Area' f. 'Baba Atal's Temple- lake view' Beato arrived in India in 1858 intending to document the uprising which had taken place the previous year. He arrived almost too late and instead focused on the aftermath, most notably the destruction of many of the monuments of Lucknow. He later travelled to Amritsar where he was the second known photographer of the Harmandir or 'Golden Temple'. He produced the first known photographic study of the temple complex. He is recorded as having produced a series of 19 images, many of which depict architectural details of the temple complex which are now lost. It appears that Beato did not produce many prints of his Amritsar series as he probably thought they would be less commercial than his images of the destruction wrought in the wake of the 1857 uprising. Note: The Golden Temple of Amritsar is known natively amongst the Sikhs as the 'Darbar Sahib' or 'Harmandir Sahib'. Dating: According to Davinder Singh Toor, Felice Beato captured his Amritsari photographs in October, 1859. Refer to: [1]",
